\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Monadic Helpers for
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}}{Monadic Helpers for }}\label{ch21-monadic-helpers-for-stdoptional}

Chapter 20 introduced
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
a thin wrapper over
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that exposes \texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{filter}, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} as member functions.
That wrapper is convenient when a value originates inside MuDSL code,
but it is not always the right shape for the value at hand. A great deal
of real code already holds a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
returned by the standard library, by a third-party API, or by a
colleague's function, and rewrapping such a value into a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} just
to call one combinator is noisy.

This chapter documents the second entry point into MuDSL's monadic
layer: a set of free functions in namespace \texttt{dsl} that operate
directly on any optional-like object. The four functions are
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}, and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else}. They
provide exactly the same combinators as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}, but
without forcing the caller to adopt a new type.

\section{Motivation: Combinators Without
Rewrapping}\label{ch21-motivation-combinators-without-rewrapping}

\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is the canonical ``value or absence'' type in modern C++, and it is used
pervasively. Standard-library facilities such as
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}find} return an iterator, but
many lookups in user code immediately translate that iterator into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}reference\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
or a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Map accessors, configuration readers, parsers, and database probes all
naturally produce \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}. Once
such a value exists, the question is what to do with it.

The idiomatic answer in a monadic style is to chain small
transformations: map a function over the contained value, flat\_map a
function that itself may fail, filter against a predicate, and supply a
default. Doing this by hand with
\texttt{if (\allowbreak{}opt)\allowbreak{}} ladders quickly turns into
nested, indented code that hides the linear shape of the computation.
The combinators in this chapter restore that linear shape.

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 20) already offers these combinators as members, but only on
its own type. Constructing a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from an existing
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is cheap, yet it is still a syntactic speed bump: every chain start
becomes
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{opt\}.\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
instead of
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}opt,\allowbreak{} .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The free-function form removes the bump.

These helpers therefore exist for a single reason: to let monadic
composition reach into the existing universe of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} values without
requiring a wrapping conversion. They are the adapter that bridges the
standard library's optional type and MuDSL's combinator vocabulary.

\section{The Four Free Functions}\label{ch21-the-four-free-functions}

MuDSL exports four free function templates in namespace \texttt{dsl}.
Each takes an optional-like object as its first argument and a callable
as its second, and each returns a value of a type deduced from the
callable. The signatures are generic in the optional type, so any type
that supports \texttt{operator*}, \texttt{operator!}, and (for
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}) \texttt{value\_\allowbreak{}or} will
work; in practice this means
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
alike.

The four functions correspond one-to-one with the four member functions
of \texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} lifts a plain function into
optional space.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map} (the
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} combinator) handles functions that
themselves return an optional.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter} keeps or discards a value
based on a predicate.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} supplies a
fallback when the optional is empty.

Their semantics are identical to the member-function versions documented
in Chapter 20. The only difference is the call site: free functions take
the optional as an explicit first argument, where member functions take
it as \texttt{*this}. This makes them composable with raw
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} values produced
anywhere in a program.

Because the function templates deduce their return type from the
callable, no explicit template arguments are needed at the call site.
The compiler infers \texttt{U} from
\texttt{f(\allowbreak{}*opt)\allowbreak{}} for \texttt{map}, the
optional-return type for \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, the original
optional type for \texttt{filter}, and the value type for
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}. This keeps call sites terse.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map}:
Lifting a
Function}{: Lifting a Function}}\label{ch21-dslmap-lifting-a-function}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} applies a function to the
contained value when the optional is engaged, and returns an empty
optional otherwise. It is the simplest of the four combinators and the
most frequently used. Its declaration, quoted verbatim from the header,
is:

\begin{code}
template <typename Opt, typename Fn>
auto
map (const Opt &opt, Fn &&fn) -> std::optional<decltype (fn (*opt))>
{
  if (!opt)
    return std::nullopt;
  return fn (*opt);
}
\end{code}

The return type is always a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
where \texttt{U} is the return type of
\texttt{fn(\allowbreak{}*opt)\allowbreak{}}. This is true even when
\texttt{U} is itself an optional-like type;
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} does not flatten nested
optionals. That flattening is the job of
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, described below.

A short example shows the typical shape. Given an engaged optional, the
function is applied and the result is wrapped; given an empty optional,
the result is empty.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  std::optional<int> v = 7;
  auto mapped = dsl::map(v, [](int x) { return x + 1; });      // optional<int>{8}
  std::optional<int> empty;
  auto nothing = dsl::map(empty, [](int x) { return x + 1; }); // nullopt
  std::cout << mapped.value_or(0) << ' ' << nothing.value_or(-1) << '\n';
}
\end{code}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} is the right combinator when
the transformation cannot fail. A function
\texttt{int -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} double} always produces a
\texttt{double}, so wrapping its result in an optional merely propagates
absence from the input. If the transformation itself can fail, the
function should return an optional and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map} should be
used instead.

Note that the callable receives the unwrapped \texttt{T}, not the
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Inside \texttt{fn}, the value is a plain \texttt{int},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}, or whatever the optional
holds. This matches the convention used by
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map}
and by
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}transform}
in C++23.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map}:
The \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}
Combinator}{: The  Combinator}}\label{ch21-dslflat_map-the-and_then-combinator}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is the
monadic bind operator. Its callable returns an optional, and
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} flattens that result: if the input
optional is empty, the empty optional propagates directly; otherwise the
callable is invoked and its returned optional becomes the result. The
verbatim declaration is:

\begin{code}
template <typename Opt, typename Fn>
auto
flat_map (const Opt &opt, Fn &&fn) -> decltype (fn (*opt))
{
  using R = decltype (fn (*opt));
  if (!opt)
    return R{};
  return fn (*opt);
}
\end{code}

The return type is exactly the type returned by \texttt{fn}, which must
be an optional-like type default-constructible to the empty state. When
the input is empty, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} returns a
default-constructed \texttt{R\{\}} rather than invoking \texttt{fn};
this short-circuiting is the whole point of the combinator.

\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is the combinator to reach for whenever
a step in a pipeline can itself fail. Parsing a string into an integer
may fail; looking up an integer in a table may fail; converting an
integer into a valid enum may fail. Each of these steps returns an
optional, and chaining them with \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
produces a single optional that is engaged only if every step succeeded.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <string>

static dsl::Maybe<int> to_int(std::string s) {
  if (s.empty()) return {};
  return std::stoi(s);
}
static std::optional<double> reciprocal(int x) {
  if (x == 0) return std::nullopt;
  return 1.0 / x;
}

int main() {
  std::optional<std::string> input = std::string("4");
  auto out = dsl::flat_map(dsl::flat_map(input, to_int), reciprocal);
  return out ? int(*out * 10) : 0;
}
\end{code}

The distinction between \texttt{map} and \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
is the same as in Chapter 20 and bears repeating. \texttt{map} is for
total functions \texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} U};
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is for partial functions
\texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Passing a function that returns an optional to \texttt{map} yields an
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is almost never what is wanted. Passing a function that returns a
plain value to \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is a compile error,
because the return type lacks the required default-construction-to-empty
semantics.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}:
Retaining or
Discarding}{: Retaining or Discarding}}\label{ch21-dslfilter-retaining-or-discarding}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter} keeps the optional's
value when a predicate holds, and clears the optional when the predicate
fails or when the optional was already empty. Its declaration is:

\begin{code}
template <typename Opt, typename Pred>
auto
filter (const Opt &opt, Pred &&pred) -> Opt
{
  if (opt && pred (*opt))
    return opt;
  return std::nullopt;
}
\end{code}

The return type is the same optional type as the input. An engaged
optional whose value satisfies the predicate is returned unchanged; an
engaged optional whose value fails the predicate becomes empty; an empty
optional stays empty. \texttt{filter} never invokes the predicate on an
absent value, so the predicate need not handle the empty case.

\texttt{filter} is most useful as a guard inside a pipeline. After
mapping a value into a more refined form, a filter can assert an
invariant: the number is positive, the string is non-empty, the index is
in range. If the invariant fails, the pipeline short-circuits to empty.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  std::optional<int> n = 7;
  auto odd = dsl::filter(n, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 1; }); // engaged, 7
  std::optional<int> m = 8;
  auto odd2 = dsl::filter(m, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 1; }); // empty
  std::cout << odd.value_or(0) << ' ' << odd2.value_or(0) << '\n';
}
\end{code}

Because \texttt{filter} returns the same optional type it receives, it
composes cleanly with \texttt{map} and \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}.
A pipeline can interleave transformations and guards without changing
the type flowing through the chain.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else}:
Supplying a
Default}{: Supplying a Default}}\label{ch21-dslor_else-supplying-a-default}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} collapses
an optional into a plain value. If the optional is engaged, it returns
the contained value; otherwise it returns the supplied fallback. Its
declaration is:

\begin{code}
template <typename Opt, typename T>
T
or_else (const Opt &opt, T fallback)
{
  return opt.value_or (std::move (fallback));
}
\end{code}

The fallback is taken by value and moved into
\texttt{opt.\allowbreak{}value\_\allowbreak{}or}. The return type is the
value type \texttt{T}, deduced from the fallback argument; the caller is
responsible for ensuring that \texttt{T} is compatible with the
optional's contained type. In practice this means passing a fallback of
the same type as the optional's value.

\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} is the terminator of a monadic chain.
Where \texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, and \texttt{filter}
keep the computation in optional space, \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}
exits it, yielding a concrete value that the rest of the program can use
without further checking. Placing \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} at the
end of a pipeline is the conventional way to ``unwrap or default''.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  std::optional<int> v;
  std::cout << dsl::or_else(v, 42) << '\n';   // 42
  std::optional<int> w = 9;
  std::cout << dsl::or_else(w, 42) << '\n';   // 9
}
\end{code}

Because \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} returns a value rather than an
optional, it cannot be followed by another combinator in the same chain.
It is the last step. Intermediate defaults that should remain in
optional space can be expressed with \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
plus a small lambda that returns an engaged optional of the fallback.

\section{Why Free Functions}\label{ch21-why-free-functions}

The decision to expose these combinators as free functions, rather than
only as members of
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
rests on three observations about C++ code in the wild.

First, the standard library owns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}. Adding member
functions to a type the library did not author is impossible without
subclassing, and subclassing a standard-library type is a well-known
anti-pattern: the standard types have no virtual destructor and are not
designed for polymorphic deletion. Free functions extend
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} without inheriting from
it.

Second, much existing code already produces
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}. A function that
returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}Config\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
cannot have its callers rewrite every call site to return
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Config\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Free functions meet the value where it is, with no conversion required.

Third, free functions compose uniformly across optional-like types.
Because the templates are generic in \texttt{Opt}, the same
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} works on
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and any user-defined optional-like type that supports the required
operations. One combinator vocabulary covers a family of types.

The cost is a slightly more verbose call site:
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}opt,\allowbreak{} f)\allowbreak{}}
instead of \texttt{opt.\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}}.
In practice this is a minor concern, and the gained uniformity across
types outweighs it. Chapter 5 discusses the concept-based design
philosophy that motivates such generic free-function APIs elsewhere in
MuDSL.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Relationship to
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}}{Relationship to }}\label{ch21-relationship-to-maybet}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} and
the free functions are two views of the same combinators.
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map}
calls the same logic that \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map}
does; the member version simply passes \texttt{*this} as the optional.
The two layers exist so that callers can choose the entry point that
fits their value.

When a value originates as a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} ---
for instance, returned by one of MuDSL's own parsing or lookup helpers
--- the member-function form is the natural choice. When a value
originates as a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- for instance, returned by
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}find}-based logic or by a
third-party API --- the free-function form avoids a wrapping conversion.

The two forms can be mixed in a single pipeline. A
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
produced by MuDSL can be passed to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter} as a free function, or a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can be wrapped once at the start of a chain and then use member
functions thereafter. The choice is stylistic.

It is worth noting that
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
constructible from a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
so wrapping is always available when the member form is preferred. The
header doc-comment for the monadic layer shows both forms side by side:

\begin{code}
// Member form, on Maybe<T>:
auto out = dsl::Maybe<int>(5).map([](int x){ return x + 1; }).or_else(0);

// Free-function form, on std::optional<int>:
std::optional<int> v = 3;
auto out = dsl::map(v, [](int x){ return x * 2; });
\end{code}

Either style yields the same result. The chapter's examples use the
free-function form throughout, since the chapter's subject is the
free-function layer, but the two forms are interchangeable.

\section{Composition and Pipelines}\label{ch21-composition-and-pipelines}

The combinators are most powerful when chained. Each combinator returns
an optional (except \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}, which terminates the
chain), so the output of one step becomes the input to the next. A
pipeline is built by nesting calls or, equivalently, by passing the
result of one combinator as the first argument to the next.

Because the free functions take the optional as their first argument, a
chain reads naturally inside-out: the innermost call is applied first,
and each enclosing call applies the next step. This is the same shape as
function composition in the standard library, and it scales to as many
steps as needed.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <string>

static std::optional<int> parse_int(std::string s) {
  if (s.empty()) return std::nullopt;
  return std::stoi(s);
}

int main() {
  std::optional<std::string> raw = std::string("42");
  auto step = dsl::map(
                dsl::filter(
                  dsl::flat_map(raw, parse_int),
                  [](int x) { return x > 0; }),
                [](int x) { return x * 2; });
  return step.value_or(0);
}
\end{code}

A pipeline like the one above expresses a clear intent: parse, validate,
transform. Each step is a small function with a single responsibility,
and the combinators handle the absence-propagation between them. There
are no \texttt{if} ladders and no nested scopes.

When a chain grows beyond three or four steps, the inside-out nesting
becomes hard to read. At that point, breaking the chain into named
intermediate variables, or wrapping the initial value into a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} to
use the member-function form, often improves clarity. The free functions
do not penalize long chains in any way other than readability.

\section{Worked Example: Parse, Lookup,
Transform}\label{ch21-worked-example-parse-lookup-transform}

Consider a small program that reads a numeric key from a string, looks
the key up in a map, and transforms the looked-up value. Each of these
three steps can fail: the string may not be a number, the number may not
be a key, and the looked-up value may fail a validity check. The
combinators express this directly.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <map>
#include <optional>
#include <string>

static std::optional<int> parse(std::string s) {
  if (s.empty()) return std::nullopt;
  return std::stoi(s);
}

static std::optional<std::string> lookup(const std::map<int, std::string>& m, int k) {
  auto it = m.find(k);
  if (it == m.end()) return std::nullopt;
  return it->second;
}

int main() {
  std::map<int, std::string> table{{1, "alpha"}, {2, "beta"}};
  std::optional<std::string> raw = std::string("2");

  auto name = dsl::flat_map(
                dsl::flat_map(raw, parse),
                [&](int k) { return lookup(table, k); });
  return name ? int(name->size()) : 0;
}
\end{code}

The two \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} calls correspond to the two
steps that can fail. Because \texttt{lookup} returns an
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
it must be composed with \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, not
\texttt{map}; using \texttt{map} would yield an
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is a type error at the next step. Adding a transformation that
cannot fail --- say, uppercasing the result --- is a job for
\texttt{map}.

\begin{code}
auto upper = dsl::map(name, [](std::string s) {
  for (auto& c : s) c = char(std::toupper(static_cast<unsigned char>(c)));
  return s;
});
\end{code}

And adding a validity check --- say, requiring the result to be
non-empty --- is a job for \texttt{filter}.

\begin{code}
auto non_empty = dsl::filter(upper, [](const std::string& s) { return !s.empty(); });
\end{code}

The chain now has four steps, each with a clear role, and absence
propagates automatically from any of them to the final result.
Terminating with \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} yields a concrete
string.

\begin{code}
std::string result = dsl::or_else(non_empty, std::string("(none)"));
\end{code}

This example illustrates the central payoff of the monadic style: the
happy path reads as a straight line of transformations, and the unhappy
paths are handled implicitly by the combinators.

\section{Composing with Standard Library
Returns}\label{ch21-composing-with-standard-library-returns}

Many standard-library APIs yield values that fit naturally into optional
pipelines. A map lookup, as shown above, is one example. Others include
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}find} on a range (whose result is
an iterator that may be \texttt{end(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}),
configuration accessors that return
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
directly, and any function that uses
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} as a return type to
signal ``no result''.

The free functions accept any optional-like type, so values from these
diverse sources can be mixed in a single pipeline. A
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from a config reader can be \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}ped into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}Handle\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from a resource manager, then \texttt{map}ped into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}Result\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from a computation. The types flow through the chain, and absence at any
stage produces an empty final result.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
  std::vector<int> xs{1, 2, 3, 4};
  // Translate a found iterator into an optional index, then transform.
  std::optional<int> idx = [&] {
    auto it = std::find(xs.begin(), xs.end(), 3);
    if (it == xs.end()) return std::optional<int>{};
    return std::optional<int>{int(it - xs.begin())};
  }();
  auto doubled = dsl::map(idx, [](int i) { return i * 2; });
  return doubled.value_or(-1);
}
\end{code}

The pattern of ``convert a sentinel-returning API into an optional, then
chain combinators'' is a common one. The free functions make it easy to
insert a standard-library value at any point in a pipeline without
adapting its type.

When the source value is expensive to copy --- a large
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}, a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector}, or a move-only type ---
the value-category of the optional matters. The free functions take the
optional by \texttt{const\&}, so the contained value is not copied when
the optional is passed in; only the transformations applied to it
produce new values. A pipeline that maps a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} to its length, for
instance, copies no strings after the initial optional is constructed.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Defaulting with
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}}{Defaulting with }}\label{ch21-defaulting-with-or_else}

Supplying a default is the most common way to exit a monadic pipeline.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} takes the
optional and a fallback value, and returns whichever is present. Because
it returns a plain value rather than an optional, it is the natural last
step of a chain.

A default can be a sentinel, a computed fallback, or a value read from a
secondary source. When the fallback is expensive to compute, care should
be taken: \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} eagerly takes the fallback by
value, so the fallback is constructed at the call site regardless of
whether the optional is engaged. For an expensive fallback, it may be
preferable to compute it lazily or to check the optional explicitly
before constructing it.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <optional>
#include <string>

int main() {
  std::optional<std::string> cfg;
  // Eager fallback: the literal is cheap to construct.
  std::string mode = dsl::or_else(cfg, std::string("default"));
  return int(mode.size());
}
\end{code}

For a default that depends on the absence itself --- for instance,
logging that a default was used --- a small
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} followed by
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} can express the side effect while keeping
the chain shape. The combinators do not prohibit side effects in the
callables, though pure functions are preferred where practical.

\section{Pitfalls}\label{ch21-pitfalls}

Several pitfalls recur often enough to deserve explicit mention. They
are the same pitfalls that apply to
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} in
Chapter 20, restated for the free-function form.

The first is the \texttt{map} versus \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
confusion. If a step's callable returns an optional,
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is required; using \texttt{map} instead
nests the optional one level deeper and produces a type that the next
step cannot consume. The compiler will usually catch this, since an
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
will not match a callable expecting \texttt{T}, but the diagnostic can
be obscure. The rule is simple: if the callable returns an optional, use
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}; otherwise use \texttt{map}.

The second pitfall is passing a callable that expects an optional rather
than the unwrapped value. The combinators invoke the callable with
\texttt{*opt}, so the callable receives a \texttt{T}, not an
\texttt{optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. A
lambda written as
\texttt{[](\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} o)\allowbreak{}\{ .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{} \}}
will fail to compile when passed to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} on a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

The third pitfall concerns value categories and copies. The optional is
taken by \texttt{const\&}, and \texttt{*opt} yields a
\texttt{const T\&}. The callable therefore receives a const reference to
the contained value. If the callable needs to mutate or move the value,
it must accept a copy; the original optional retains its value. For
move-only types, this means the value cannot be moved out through the
combinators without an explicit copy or a non-const wrapper.

The fourth pitfall is the eager evaluation of the
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} fallback, discussed above. The fallback
is constructed at the call site before \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}
decides whether to use it. For cheap fallbacks this is invisible; for
expensive ones it is a hidden cost.

The fifth pitfall is mixing the free-function and member-function forms
inconsistently within a single pipeline. While the two forms
interoperate without error, a chain that switches between
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}opt,\allowbreak{} f)\allowbreak{}}
and \texttt{opt.\allowbreak{}map(\allowbreak{}f)\allowbreak{}} is harder
to read than one that sticks to a single style. Consistency aids
readers.

Finally, the combinators are not a substitute for error reporting. An
empty optional carries no information about which step failed. When the
location of a failure matters,
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22) is the appropriate type: it propagates an error value
rather than mere absence. The optional combinators are best suited to
cases where any failure is equivalent --- the answer is either present
or not --- and where the cost of distinguishing failures is not
justified.

\section{Summary}\label{ch21-summary}

MuDSL provides four free-function combinators ---
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}, and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} --- that
extend monadic composition to any optional-like type, with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as the primary target. They mirror exactly the four member functions of
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 20), giving users two entry points into the same combinator
vocabulary: a wrapper type for values that originate inside MuDSL, and
free functions for values that arrive as raw
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} from the standard
library or third-party code.

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} lifts a total function
\texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} U} into optional space.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map} binds a
partial function
\texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
flattening the result and short-circuiting on absence.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter} retains a value only when
a predicate holds.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} collapses
an optional to a plain value, supplying a fallback when the optional is
empty. Together, they let a pipeline of parse-validate-transform steps
be written as a straight line, with absence propagated implicitly
between stages.

The free functions are generic in the optional type, so they work
uniformly on \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional},
\texttt{Maybe}, and any user-defined optional-like type that supports
the required operations. They avoid subclassing the standard type, they
meet existing values where they are, and they compose cleanly with the
output of standard-library APIs such as map lookups and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}find}. The same caveats apply as
in Chapter 20: choose \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} when the callable
returns an optional, remember that the callable receives the unwrapped
value, and prefer
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22) when the location of a failure must be reported rather than
merely its presence.
